      body: `## The second creation story

After the cosmic order of Genesis 1, the Bible tells a second story of creation, in a very different style (Genesis 2:4b–3:24). Read it before continuing.

> "And the LORD God formed man of the dust of the ground, and breathed into his nostrils the breath of life; and man became a living soul." (Genesis 2:7, King James Version)

The Hebrew contains a pun that English cannot capture: the **man** (*adam*) is formed from the **ground** (*adamah*). He is, as we might say, an "earthling." The word *adam* is not yet a proper name; it means "human being," and only gradually, in chapters 4 and 5, becomes the name Adam. The man becomes a "living soul" (*nefesh hayyah*), a living being, when God breathes into him: human life is dust animated by the breath of God.

## The garden

God plants a **garden** "eastward in Eden," with every tree that is pleasant to the sight and good for food, and in the middle of it two special trees: the **tree of life**, and the **tree of the knowledge of good and evil**. A river flows out of Eden and divides into four, including the **Tigris** and the **Euphrates**, which places the garden, in the story's imagination, somewhere in Mesopotamia. God puts the man in the garden "to dress it and to keep it," and gives him a single command:

> "Of every tree of the garden thou mayest freely eat: But of the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, thou shalt not eat of it: for in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die." (Genesis 2:16–17)

## The woman

Then God says, "It is not good that the man should be alone; I will make him an help meet for him" (2:18). The phrase "help meet" (*ezer kenegdo*) means a helper "corresponding to him" or "suitable for him"; the word *ezer*, "helper," is used elsewhere of God himself (Psalm 121:1–2), and does not imply inferiority. God forms the animals and brings them to the man to name, but none is a fitting partner. So God casts a deep sleep on the man, takes one of his **ribs** (or "sides"), and builds it into a **woman**. The man's first words are a poem:

> "This is now bone of my bones, and flesh of my flesh: she shall be called Woman (*ishah*), because she was taken out of Man (*ish*)." (Genesis 2:23)

The narrator adds: "Therefore shall a man leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave unto his wife: and they shall be one flesh. And they were both naked, the man and his wife, and were not ashamed" (2:24–25).

## The serpent

Then the **serpent**, "more subtil than any beast of the field," speaks to the woman (3:1). There is another pun: the man and woman were **naked** (*arummim*), and the serpent is **subtle** (*arum*). The serpent asks: "Yea, hath God said, Ye shall not eat of every tree of the garden?" The woman replies that they may eat of the trees, except the one in the middle, "neither shall ye touch it, lest ye die," adding a prohibition on touching that God did not give. The serpent answers: "Ye shall not surely die: For God doth know that in the day ye eat thereof, then your eyes shall be opened, and ye shall be as gods, knowing good and evil" (3:4–5).

> "And when the woman saw that the tree was good for food, and that it was pleasant to the eyes, and a tree to be desired to make one wise, she took of the fruit thereof, and did eat, and gave also unto her husband with her; and he did eat. And the eyes of them both were opened, and they knew that they were naked; and they sewed fig leaves together, and made themselves aprons." (Genesis 3:6–7)

Notice that the fruit is never identified. The tradition that it was an **apple** comes from Latin, where *malum* means both "apple" and "evil."

## The consequences

God walks in the garden "in the cool of the day," and the man and woman hide. "Where art thou?" God asks (3:9). The man blames the woman, "whom thou gavest to be with me"; the woman blames the serpent. God pronounces sentences on each (3:14–19). The serpent will crawl on its belly and eat dust, and there will be enmity between its offspring and the woman's. The woman will bear children in pain, "and thy desire shall be to thy husband, and he shall rule over thee." The man will toil to wring food from a cursed ground, "in the sweat of thy face," until he returns to the ground: "for dust thou art, and unto dust shalt thou return."

God makes them garments of skins, and expels them from the garden, "lest he put forth his hand, and take also of the tree of life, and eat, and live for ever." He places **cherubim** and a flaming sword at the east of Eden, to guard the way to the tree of life (3:22–24).

## What kind of story is this?

The story of Eden is a story of **origins**, explaining why things are as they are: why snakes crawl, why childbirth is painful, why farming is hard, why men and women are drawn to each other, why human beings wear clothes and feel shame, and, above all, why we **die**. Like other ancient stories, it uses symbols: a talking snake, magical trees, a garden guarded by cherubim. Its meaning has been debated for millennia.

What is the "**knowledge of good and evil**"? Interpreters have suggested moral knowledge, the ability to distinguish right from wrong; sexual knowledge, since the couple's first discovery is their nakedness; or knowledge of **everything**, "good and evil" being a figure of speech for the whole range of things, as in other biblical passages. The serpent's promise, "ye shall be as gods," suggests that the sin is a grasping after a **divine** status, a refusal to accept the limits of creaturehood.

Did God lie when he said that "in the day that thou eatest thereof thou shalt surely die"? The man and woman do not die that day. Some interpreters say they became **mortal**; others that they died spiritually; others that the death sentence was mercifully postponed. The story leaves the question open.

> **Pause and consider.** When God asks the man what he has done, the man blames the woman and God ("the woman whom thou gavest to be with me"), and the woman blames the serpent. What does the story suggest about human beings' response to their own wrongdoing?`,
