      body: `## The "Fall"

In Christian tradition, the story of Eden is known as the **Fall**: the moment when humanity fell from a state of innocence into sin and death. The apostle **Paul** wrote: "By one man sin entered into the world, and death by sin; and so death passed upon all men, for that all have sinned" (Romans 5:12). **Augustine** (354–430) developed this into the doctrine of **original sin**: all human beings inherit from Adam a corrupted nature, inclined to sin, and share in his guilt. This doctrine, central to Western Christianity, shaped the theology of Aquinas, Luther, and Calvin. Eastern Christian traditions have generally emphasized inherited mortality and weakness rather than inherited guilt.

**Judaism** has generally read the story differently. The Hebrew Bible itself never refers back to the Eden story as the origin of human sinfulness; Adam is barely mentioned after Genesis 5. Rabbinic tradition taught that every human being has both a good inclination (*yetzer ha-tov*) and an evil inclination (*yetzer ha-ra*), and is free to choose; sin is real and universal, but not an inherited condition. "Sin lieth at the door," God tells Cain, "and unto thee shall be his desire, and thou shalt rule over him" (Genesis 4:7): sin is a danger to be mastered, not a fate. Some later Jewish texts, such as 2 Esdras (4 Ezra, late first century CE), do lament that Adam's sin brought death to his descendants (7:118).

## Eve

The story has had a long and troubled history in the treatment of **women**. The New Testament letter 1 Timothy cites it to argue that women should not teach or have authority over men, "for Adam was first formed, then Eve. And Adam was not deceived, but the woman being deceived was in the transgression" (1 Timothy 2:13–14). The early Christian writer **Tertullian** (c. 160–225) called women "the devil's gateway" (*On the Apparel of Women* 1.1). Such readings were used for centuries to justify the subordination of women.

Modern interpreters, especially **feminist** scholars such as **Phyllis Trible**, in *God and the Rhetoric of Sexuality* (1978), have read the story differently. They point out that the man and woman are created as **partners**; that the man is "with her" when she eats (3:6), and silent; that the woman, not the man, engages the serpent in theological discussion, and is drawn by the desire for **wisdom**; and that the rule of the husband over the wife is presented as a **consequence** of the disobedience, a description of a broken world, not a command of how things should be. Others, such as **Carol Meyers**, have studied the story against the background of the hard life of Israelite farming families, in which women's labor and childbearing were both essential.

The name **Eve** (*Havvah*) is given to the woman by the man only after the expulsion, "because she was the mother of all living" (3:20); the name is related to the Hebrew word for "life."

## Cain and Abel

Outside Eden, the man and woman have two sons: **Cain**, a farmer, and **Abel**, a shepherd (Genesis 4). Both bring offerings to God; God accepts Abel's, from the firstlings of his flock, but not Cain's, from the fruit of the ground. The story does not say why. Cain is very angry, and his face falls. God warns him:

> "If thou doest well, shalt thou not be accepted? and if thou doest not well, sin lieth at the door. And unto thee shall be his desire, and thou shalt rule over him." (Genesis 4:7)

Cain speaks to his brother, and when they are in the field, he kills him: the first murder, a **fratricide**. God asks, "Where is Abel thy brother?" Cain replies with one of the most famous lines in the Bible: "**I know not: Am I my brother's keeper?**" (4:9). God answers: "What hast thou done? the voice of thy brother's blood crieth unto me from the ground" (4:10).

Cain is cursed, and condemned to be "a fugitive and a vagabond in the earth." When he protests that whoever finds him will kill him, God puts a **mark** on him, so that no one will kill him (4:15). The "mark of Cain" was later, wrongly, interpreted as a curse, and even used to justify racism. In the story, it is a sign of God's **protection**. Cain goes east of Eden, to the land of **Nod** ("wandering"), and builds the first **city**.

## The spread of violence

The story of Cain's descendants shows the growth of civilization and of violence together: Jabal, the ancestor of herdsmen; Jubal, of musicians; Tubal-cain, of metalworkers; and **Lamech**, who boasts to his wives: "I have slain a man to my wounding, and a young man to my hurt. If Cain shall be avenged sevenfold, truly Lamech seventy and sevenfold" (4:23–24). Jesus, when asked how often one should forgive, answered, "seventy times seven" (Matthew 18:22), perhaps reversing Lamech's boast of unlimited revenge into unlimited forgiveness.

Genesis 5 then traces the descendants of Adam through **Seth**, his third son, in a genealogy with fantastically long lifespans: **Methuselah** lives 969 years. Similar long reigns appear in the Sumerian King List, whose kings before the flood ruled for tens of thousands of years. Then comes a strange fragment: the "sons of God" saw that "the daughters of men" were fair, and took them as wives, and their children were the **Nephilim**, "mighty men which were of old, men of renown" (6:1–4). The story is unexplained, but it seems to mark the breaking of the boundary between the divine and the human, and leads directly to the flood.

## Why was Abel's offering accepted?

The story's silence about why God accepted Abel's offering and not Cain's has provoked many explanations. Some ancient interpreters said that Abel offered the **best** of his flock, "the firstlings … and the fat thereof" (4:4), while Cain offered merely "of the fruit of the ground," not the first fruits. The letter to the Hebrews says that Abel offered "by faith" (Hebrews 11:4), and 1 John that Cain's "own works were evil" (1 John 3:12). Some modern readers see the preference of the shepherd over the farmer as reflecting an old tension between nomadic and settled ways of life. Others think the silence is the point: God's choice is not explained, and the question is not why Abel was chosen, but how Cain will respond to not being chosen, a theme that runs through all of Genesis, where God repeatedly chooses the younger brother over the elder.

> **Pause and consider.** Cain asks, "Am I my brother's keeper?" The story implies the answer is yes. What does it mean to be responsible for another person? How far does that responsibility extend?`,
