      body: `## The promise fulfilled

In Genesis 21, at last, "the LORD visited Sarah as he had said," and she bears **Isaac** in her old age. "God hath made me to laugh," she says, "so that all that hear will laugh with me" (21:6). Then, at Sarah's demand, Abraham sends Hagar and Ishmael away into the wilderness, where, when the water runs out, Hagar puts the boy under a bush and sits at a distance, "for she said, Let me not see the death of the child." God hears the boy's voice and shows her a well, and promises that Ishmael too will become a great nation (21:9–21).

The promise of descendants has been fulfilled through Isaac. And then comes the most terrifying test in the Bible.

## The test

> "And it came to pass after these things, that God did tempt Abraham, and said unto him, Abraham: and he said, Behold, here I am. And he said, Take now thy son, thine only son Isaac, whom thou lovest, and get thee into the land of Moriah; and offer him there for a burnt offering upon one of the mountains which I will tell thee of." (Genesis 22:1–2, King James Version)

Read the whole story, Genesis 22:1–19, before continuing. It is only nineteen verses long.

The story is called, in Jewish tradition, the ***Akedah***, "the **binding**," because Abraham binds Isaac on the altar. Notice how the command builds: "thy son, thine only son Isaac, whom thou lovest." Each phrase tightens the knot. And notice the echo of chapter 12: there, Abraham was told "Get thee out" (*lekh lekha*) from his father's house, his past; here, the same words, "get thee" (*lekh lekha*) to the land of Moriah, ask him to give up his son, his future.

## The silence

What strikes every reader is what the story does **not** say. Abraham does not argue, as he argued for Sodom. He rises early in the morning, saddles his ass, cuts the wood, and goes. We are not told what he thought or felt, or what he said to Sarah. As Erich Auerbach observed, in the essay you read about in lesson 1, the story is "fraught with background": its meaning lies in silence.

On the third day, Abraham sees the place afar off, and tells his servants to wait: "I and the lad will go yonder and worship, and come again to you" (22:5). Does he believe they will both return? Or is he concealing the truth? The text does not say. Abraham lays the wood on Isaac, and takes the fire and the knife himself, "and they went both of them together" (22:6).

Then comes the only dialogue between father and son:

> "And Isaac spake unto Abraham his father, and said, My father: and he said, Here am I, my son. And he said, Behold the fire and the wood: but where is the lamb for a burnt offering? And Abraham said, My son, God will provide himself a lamb for a burnt offering: so they went both of them together." (Genesis 22:7–8)

The repetition, "so they went both of them together," frames the exchange. Abraham's answer, "God will provide," is ambiguous: is it faith, evasion, or prophecy?

## The ram

At the place, Abraham builds the altar, lays the wood in order, binds Isaac, and lays him on the altar on the wood. He stretches out his hand, and takes the knife, to slay his son. Then the angel of the LORD calls from heaven, "Abraham, Abraham": "Lay not thine hand upon the lad, … for now I know that thou fearest God, seeing thou hast not withheld thy son, thine only son from me" (22:11–12). Abraham sees a **ram** caught in a thicket by its horns, and offers it instead of his son. He names the place "**The LORD will provide**" (*YHWH yireh*, 22:14). The angel renews the promises: because Abraham has obeyed, his descendants will be as the stars of heaven and the sand on the seashore, "and in thy seed shall all the nations of the earth be blessed" (22:18).

## Interpretations

The *Akedah* has been interpreted in many ways.

**Against child sacrifice.** Some scholars read the story as a rejection of **child sacrifice**, which was practiced by some of Israel's neighbors and, the prophets say, by some Israelites (2 Kings 16:3; Jeremiah 7:31). God demands the sacrifice, but then forbids it and provides an animal instead. The God of Israel does not want human sacrifice.

**The test of faith.** In Jewish tradition, the binding is the supreme example of Abraham's **faith** and **obedience**, and of the merit of the fathers, which God remembers in favor of their descendants. The ram's horn, the **shofar**, blown at the New Year festival of Rosh Hashanah, recalls the ram caught in the thicket, and the *Akedah* is read in the synagogue on that day. Later midrash identified Mount Moriah with the Temple Mount in Jerusalem (as 2 Chronicles 3:1 already does). Some midrashim imagine that Isaac was a grown man, who consented to be bound; others, written after the massacres of Jewish communities in the Rhineland during the First Crusade in 1096, told that Isaac was actually killed and brought back to life, and compared the martyrs who killed their own children rather than accept forced baptism to Abraham.

**A type of Christ.** Christian interpreters saw Isaac as a **type** of Christ: the beloved son, carrying the wood of his own sacrifice up the hill, as Jesus carried his cross; and the ram as a type of Christ, the lamb God provides. The letter to the Hebrews says that Abraham "accounted that God was able to raise him up, even from the dead" (Hebrews 11:19).

**The teleological suspension of the ethical.** The Danish philosopher **Søren Kierkegaard**, in ***Fear and Trembling*** (1843), written under the pseudonym "Johannes de silentio," made the binding the center of a profound meditation on faith. Abraham, Kierkegaard argued, is either a murderer or a "knight of faith," who believes, "by virtue of the absurd," that he will both sacrifice Isaac and receive him back. His faith involves a "**teleological suspension of the ethical**": a higher obligation to God that overrides the universal ethical duty of a father to his son, and that cannot be explained or justified to others. Kierkegaard's narrator confessed that he could not understand Abraham, and could learn nothing from him except to be amazed.

**A moral horror.** Others, such as the philosopher **Immanuel Kant**, argued that Abraham should have answered God: "That I ought not to kill my good son is quite certain. But that you, this apparition, are God, of that I am not certain" (*The Conflict of the Faculties*, 1798). For Kant, no command to commit murder could come from God.

## Where is Sarah?

After the binding, Abraham returns to his servants, and they go to Beersheba. Isaac is not mentioned as returning with him (22:19). The next chapter begins with the death of **Sarah** (23:1–2). Some rabbinic interpreters connected the two: Sarah died when she heard what Abraham had done. The text leaves us to wonder.

> **Pause and consider.** Kierkegaard said that Abraham was either a murderer or a "knight of faith." Kant said that Abraham should have doubted that the voice was God's. How would you read the story? What does it ask of its readers?`,
